# Solution

**Answer: Yes.** Taking $G$-invariants is an exact functor on $R[G]$-modules when $|G|=n$ is invertible in $R$.

## Proof

Let $(-)^G : R[G]\text{-Mod} \to R\text{-Mod}$ be the invariant functor, sending a module $M$ to
$$M^G = \{m \in M : g\cdot m = m \text{ for all } g \in G\}.$$

Since $n = |G|$ is invertible in $R$, define the **averaging operator** (Reynolds operator)
$$\pi_M : M \to M, \qquad \pi_M(m) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{g \in G} g\cdot m.$$

**Claim 1:** $\pi_M(m) \in M^G$ for all $m \in M$.

For any $h \in G$:
$$h \cdot \pi_M(m) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{g \in G} hg \cdot m = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{g' \in G} g' \cdot m = \pi_M(m),$$
using that left-multiplication by $h$ permutes $G$. So $\pi_M$ maps into $M^G$.

**Claim 2:** $\pi_M$ restricts to the identity on $M^G$.

If $m \in M^G$, then $g \cdot m = m$ for all $g$, so $\pi_M(m) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{g} m = m$.

Thus $\pi_M : M \to M^G$ is a retraction of the inclusion $\iota_M : M^G \hookrightarrow M$, i.e., $\pi_M \circ \iota_M = \mathrm{id}_{M^G}$.

**Claim 3 (Naturality):** For any $R[G]$-linear map $\varphi : M \to N$, the diagram
$$\begin{array}{ccc} M & \xrightarrow{\pi_M} & M^G \\ \downarrow{\varphi} & & \downarrow{\varphi|_{M^G}} \\ N & \xrightarrow{\pi_N} & N^G \end{array}$$
commutes. Indeed,
$$\varphi(\pi_M(m)) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_g \varphi(g \cdot m) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_g g \cdot \varphi(m) = \pi_N(\varphi(m)).$$

**Exactness.** Consider a short exact sequence of $R[G]$-modules
$$0 \to A \xrightarrow{f} B \xrightarrow{p} C \to 0.$$

Applying $(-)^G$ always gives left exactness, so we have
$$0 \to A^G \xrightarrow{f} B^G \xrightarrow{p} C^G$$
exact. It remains to show $p : B^G \to C^G$ is **surjective**.

Let $c \in C^G$. By surjectivity of $p : B \to C$, choose $b \in B$ with $p(b) = c$. Set
$$\bar{b} = \pi_B(b) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{g \in G} g \cdot b \in B^G.$$
Then by naturality (Claim 3),
$$p(\bar{b}) = p(\pi_B(b)) = \pi_C(p(b)) = \pi_C(c) = c,$$
where the last equality uses $c \in C^G$ and Claim 2. Hence every $c \in C^G$ has a preimage in $B^G$.

Therefore
$$0 \to A^G \to B^G \to C^G \to 0$$
is exact, and $(-)^G$ is exact. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** This is the module-theoretic shadow of Maschke's theorem: $n$ invertible in $R$ makes $R[G]$ semisimple, and over a semisimple ring every module is projective/injective, so every additive functor—particularly $(-)^G \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{R[G]}(R, -)$—is exact. Equivalently, $\mathrm{Ext}^i_{R[G]}(R, M) = 0$ for $i > 0$, i.e., $|G|$ invertible $\Rightarrow$ $H^i(G; M) = 0$ for $i > 0$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
